My experience with the convict today (all humans):

Wizard mode testing showed that a wish is not that reliable for the
convict early in the game…  ;-)

1. Got killed before even moving.  10HP isn't too much, and the tourist
at least has something to hit with…  ;-)  Should have picked up the ball
and hit with it and/or ran away, I now realize.

2. Pretty good start; two food rations and a wand of speed monster in
the starting room, AC2 reached in dlvl 2 (rat-testing all armor), wand
of opening found to deal with the chain, shirt covered, large general
store there (WW boots, a 50zm helmet, a lv1 and a lv2 spellbook and
several other goodies I couldn't yet affort; vault there yet ignored).
Killed by a floating eye on dlvl 3 – lack of infravision and that _one_
keystroke too much.  YASD, in other words.  I'd had enough daggers
already and that skill at basic.

3. Starved pretty soon; just didn't find enough food.  Tried praying
when I was as good as dead, didn't help me either.  Of course.  ;-)

4. Found enough food on dlvl 1.  Pet got killed very soon.  Explored the
level well, then went down to dlvl 2 – half of my 14 HP gone by getting
hit by the ball, the other half in the next move by a wand of striking I
couldn't see coming.

Generally:  I usually get most weaker roles down to ~ dlvl 3-4 without
problems (ascended a wizard and a tourist so far), so IMHO your goal of
making the (very) early game harder has been reached.  Out of four
games, there was only one in which I would have felt confident to
continue further down.  Since I tend to forget about the tourist's
camera sooner or later, I'd probably have played it in a similar way
from then.  I like the additional decisions involved; especially the
lack of a halfway decent weapon and the weak starting pet.

Eating zombies and suchlike without regret is a major advantage for the
convict, especially regarding his shortcomings.  I also like eating the
lichens the rat sometimes scatters around in dungeon without having to
consider their age.  The strength helps carrying the iron ball, and is a
definite advantage once you get rid of it.

BTW, I didn't encounter any rats yet – are they peaceful to the convict
by default?  I'd like that; it could, when they occur, repeatedly lead
to the decision of an urgently needed snack vs. an even more negative
alignment record, thereby prolonging the "early" phase.  Also, taming
(growable) rats with food might be an option available to the convict
only. They are mostly useful early, when food is terribly sparse,
thereby offering another interesting choice to the role.  OTOH, easy
access to rodents of unusual size for other roles might be somewhat
unbalancing, so I'd restrict food taming of rats to convicts.  (But they
might become peaceful for others.)

Tell me what you think.

